# First Caller

**Odoo 19's database manager — the surface that can back up, restore, or delete every company database on an instance — is reachable without authentication, ships with its master password set to `admin`, and hands that password to the first caller who shows up with a replacement. On the official image, set up the documented way, we enumerated the databases unauthenticated, replaced the master password with our own, and downloaded the whole company.**
**knell — 2026-11-04**

- ---

## Summary

Every route under `/web/database/*` in Odoo 19 is declared `auth="none"`. The POST routes are `csrf=False`. The routes are: selector, manager, create, duplicate, drop, backup, restore, change_password, and list. This is the database manager — the component that creates, clones, backs up, restores, and deletes the PostgreSQL databases that hold entire companies: their ledgers, their payroll, their attachments, their users.

Two defaults decide what that surface is worth in the world.

The first is that database listing ships on. The `--no-database-list` option is a `store_false` flag whose default is True, so unless an operator explicitly sets `list_db = False`, `POST /web/database/list` returns the instance's database names to anyone who asks. No credential, no master password — the service layer's own section header for these functions reads "No master password required."

The second is the master password itself. `admin_passwd` is a file-only configuration option whose code default is the literal string `admin`. The official Docker image does not override it: its shipped `odoo.conf` carries the setting only as a commented-out line, so the code default rules on a default deployment. And while the password is still `admin`, every mutating manager call runs a bootstrap rule: if the caller submitted any `master_pwd` at all, the server silently replaces the master password with the caller's value and proceeds with the operation under it. The manager page displays a warning that it is not protected. A warning, not a block.

The composition is the finding. An unauthenticated caller learns the database names, then — on any instance still carrying the shipped password — takes the master password itself with the first mutating call, and with it the ability to download a full clone of every company on the box, restore an uploaded database in its place, or delete the databases and their file stores outright. One static secret, server-wide, not per-database.

This is not a dormant surface. A write-up of the October 2026 cryptominer campaign records that the attacker's first request against every compromised server was `POST /web/database/list` — the enumeration call — because `list_db` had never been set False. That campaign is prior art and is credited as such; what this paper adds is the current-pin read of the whole construction and a live demonstration of the bootstrap on the official image.

The proof was performed only against our own instance: the official `odoo:19` image with `postgres:16-alpine`, the documented compose pattern, no configuration hardening, localhost only. Every live claim below is tagged as such; the rest were read at the deciding lines of the pinned source. The pin is Odoo Community 19.0, branch `19.0` at `3828306014f63c07b769f0fb3cfce5eb7f7c50d3`.

This paper also records what Odoo gets right, because it gets several hard things right that other products in this audit series get wrong. Its API keys are properly hashed at rest. Its session construction is the strongest seen in this series: a password change kills other sessions by construction. Its attachment serving is sound, and its module-upload path cannot install code at all. The failures are specific, and they are stated specifically.

## 1. The door is listed

The manager's route table is the starting point. In `addons/web/controllers/database.py`, every route — selector, manager, create, duplicate, drop, backup, restore, change_password, list — is `auth="none"`, and the POST routes set `csrf=False`. Authentication for the mutating operations is meant to come from the master password alone, checked inside the handlers. Enumeration does not even get that.

`POST /web/database/list` returns `http.db_list()` whenever listing is enabled. Listing is enabled by default: in `odoo/tools/config.py`, `--no-database-list` is declared `store_false` with destination `list_db` and `my_default=True`. Only an operator who has read the deployment documentation and acted on it — setting `list_db = False` or passing the flag — turns it off. The selector and manager pages render the same database list into a public page, so the names also leak through the login flow's database picker.

The service layer adds nothing. Its gate, `check_db_management_enabled`, is exactly "if not list_db: AccessDenied." The functions `exp_db_exist`, `exp_list`, and `exp_server_version` require no master password at all.

Live, on the rig: before any database existed, `POST /web/database/list` with no credentials returned HTTP 200 and `{"result": []}`. After one database was created, the same call returned `{"result": ["finisherdb"]}`.

The wild corroboration belongs to others and is credited here rather than claimed: the October 2026 cryptominer campaign's operators used this exact call as their first request against every server they compromised, to learn the database name, because the servers they found had never set `list_db` False. A default that attackers enumerate first is not a theoretical default.

## 2. The password is `admin`, and the first caller replaces it

The master password's custody begins at `odoo/tools/config.py`: `admin_passwd` is a `FileOnlyOption` with `my_default='admin'`. Unless the configuration file sets a value, the master password for the instance is the string `admin`. Once a password is set through the proper path, custody is respectable: `set_admin_password()` stores a pbkdf2_sha512 hash via the crypt context, `exp_change_admin_password` persists it into the config file, and `verify_admin_password()` auto-upgrades the stored hash after a successful verification. The weakness is not the hashing. It is the value the hashing protects on a default install, and what the code does while that value stands.

Every mutating manager controller — create, duplicate, drop, backup, restore — begins with the same bootstrap:

```python
insecure = odoo.tools.config.verify_admin_password('admin')
if insecure and master_pwd:
    dispatch_rpc('db', 'change_admin_password', ["admin", master_pwd])
```

Read it plainly. If the master password still verifies as the shipped default `admin`, and the caller submitted any `master_pwd` value, the server first changes the master password to the caller's value, then proceeds with the requested operation under the new password. There is no confirmation, no existing-credential proof beyond the public default, no logging distinction between this and a legitimate change. The manager page renders an `insecure` flag — "your Odoo database manager is not protected" — as a banner. The banner does not block anything.

The design intent is visible: a first-run convenience so the operator can set a real password. The effect, on any reachable instance whose operator never set one, is that the master password is not `admin` for whoever arrives first. It is *theirs*.

The official image makes this the common case rather than the edge case. Its shipped `/etc/odoo/odoo.conf` carries `; admin_passwd = admin` — commented out. The documented compose pattern sets no master-password environment variable. An operator who follows the official quick-start and does not separately study the deployment-hardening documentation runs with the code default in force and `list_db` at its default too.

Live, on the rig, the bootstrap fired exactly as read. A `POST /web/database/create` call carrying a caller-chosen `master_pwd`, made while the password was still the default, created the database and silently replaced the master password. The evidence is on disk and behavioral both:

- - After the call, the container's `odoo.conf` held an active, persisted line `admin_passwd = $pbkdf2-sha512$600000$…` where the commented default had been — the bootstrap's `change_admin_password` → config-save persistence, observed in the file.
- - The "not protected" warning was present on the manager page before the bootstrap and absent after it.
- - `POST /web/database/change_password` presenting the old `admin` returned "Master password update error: Access Denied."
- - `POST /web/database/backup` presenting `admin` returned "Database backup error: Access Denied" — no archive.
- - `POST /web/database/drop` presenting the caller's new password returned HTTP 303 and dropped the database.

A NixOS packaging variant of this same default turned critical as **CVE-2026-25137**, when the master password there could not persist and the bootstrap therefore stayed open permanently. That CVE is owned prior art and is credited. The generic construction — shipped default plus first-caller replacement — is the upstream design at this pin, and it is what the live proof exercised.

## 3. What one password buys

With `check_super` passed, the one static, server-wide secret gates four operations over every database on the instance:

- - **Backup.** `dump_db()` streams a zip containing `dump.sql` — a full `pg_dump` — plus a copytree of the entire `filestore/`, which is every attachment the company has uploaded, plus `manifest.json`. A read-clone of the whole company, delivered to whoever holds the password.
- - **Restore.** An uploaded zip is unpacked for its known members — `dump.sql` and `filestore/*` — the SQL is run through `psql`, and the filestore is moved into place. The restored database is a complete company clone, including its users, their password hashes, and their API-key hashes. Whoever holds the master password can replace a live company with a database of their choosing.
- - **Drop.** The PostgreSQL database is deleted and its filestore removed from disk.
- - **Create, duplicate, rename.** Arbitrary new databases; create additionally logs the caller in as the new database's administrator, under a password the caller chose.

The backup was exercised end-to-end on the rig. The test database was seeded with a sentinel partner record and a sentinel attachment. `POST /web/database/backup` with the bootstrapped password returned HTTP 200, `application/octet-stream`, **531,593 bytes**: a zip of 24 members — `dump.sql`, `manifest.json` (database `finisherdb`, version 19.0-20261102), and 22 `filestore/` files. The dump opens with the pg_dump header and the `\restrict` preamble line. One password, one request, the whole company.

The structural point is the singularity of the secret. The master password is not per-database, not per-company, not rotatable per tenant. Multi-company hosting on one instance — the ordinary way Odoo is deployed by the small firms that serve other small firms — concentrates every tenant behind this one value, and behind its shipped default until someone changes it.

## 4. API keys: well-kept at rest, never revoked

The API-key construction shows what this codebase does when it is being careful, which is why the exceptions stand out.

Generation is `binascii.hexlify(os.urandom(20))` — a 160-bit key from the operating system's CSPRNG, shown once. Storage is hashed: a pbkdf2_sha512 crypt context at 6000 rounds, with only the first eight hex characters kept in plaintext as a lookup index. A database read does not yield usable keys. This is credited without qualification; it is better custody than several larger products in this series manage. On the rig, the minted test key's row confirmed the shape live: stored value `$pbkdf2-sha512$6000$…`, lookup index present, nothing recoverable.

Three decisions undo much of that care in practice.

**The default key is unscoped.** A key's scope check is `(scope IS NULL OR scope = requested)` — a NULL scope passes every check. Keys minted through the Preferences UI are created with `scope=None` unless a caller specifies otherwise. The default key a user generates is therefore generic RPC, and because the XML-RPC, JSON-RPC, and JSON-2 APIs authenticate *as the user* — an API key sits in the password slot and executes with the account's full rights and record rules — an unscoped key is not a limited integration token. It is the account. The test key's row on the rig showed `scope` NULL, as read.

**Expiry is policy for users and optional for the system.** Ordinary users must set an expiration, capped by their groups — the base internal group's `api_key_duration` is 90 days. The system path is exempt: `_check_expiration_date` returns early for a system caller, and the generation code documents the result plainly — "for a persistent key (infinite duration), no value for expiration date." Persistent, never-expiring API keys exist by design on the administrative path.

**A password change revokes nothing.** `_change_password()` sets the new password and stops. Nothing in the password-change or password-reset path touches `res_users_apikeys`. A key minted before a change keeps working after it, until its own expiry — which, for a persistent key, is never — or until someone finds it and removes it by hand.

Live, the sequence ran on the rig in order: a key was minted for a fresh internal user; the key authenticated and executed `res.partner.search_count`; the user changed password through the normal `res.users.change_password` flow; the old password was dead and the new password worked; **the same key then authenticated and executed again.** A password reset is the standard remediation for a compromised account. Here it remediates the password and leaves the keys standing.

One rig note, recorded rather than smoothed over: the web wizard's key-minting method is bound to an HTTP session and refuses XML-RPC, so the test key was minted by invoking the wizard's own production generation path, `res.users.apikeys._generate(scope=None, …)`, in the user's environment on our instance. The scope, storage, and checking exercised are the identical code the UI drives.

## 5. What the sessions get right

The session construction is the strongest in this audit series, and the contrast is the point of recording it.

Session IDs are generated from `sha512(time || os.urandom(64))` — high entropy, no weak-RNG disease anywhere in the audited surface. Login rotates the session; logout and expiry rotate hard. The decisive property is the per-request session token: an HMAC-SHA256 keyed over the session's token fields, which *include the stored password hash*, together with a per-database secret. Change the password and every session token changes with it — other sessions die by construction, not because a cleanup routine remembered to delete them. Products with far larger security teams ship the opposite: password changes that revoke nothing. Odoo's construction is the correct one, and it is credited as the standard the others fail.

Reset tokens match that standard. There is no stored random string to steal: the token is an HMAC-SHA256-signed payload keyed by the per-database `database.secret`, embedding the partner, the user set, the signup type, and the last-login date, with a default validity of four hours for resets. Any login kills outstanding tokens, because the embedded login date no longer matches; a completed reset authenticates the user, making the token effectively single-use. A reset kills the user's other web sessions through the binding above. It does not kill their API keys — §4 is the exception that survives an otherwise coherent design.

One deployment bound is noted: the `session_id` cookie is set `HttpOnly`, but the set-cookie call passes no `Secure` and no `SameSite` attribute at this pin. Behind the documented HTTPS reverse-proxy deployment this is covered by the proxy; behind a plain-HTTP deployment or a proxy that adds neither attribute, the cookie travels as-is.

## 6. The sound surfaces

Two areas were read expecting the usual findings and did not produce them.

**Attachments.** `/web/content` and `/web/image` resolve through `ir_binary._find_record`. A limited field access token must verify — HMAC, scope-bound, time-windowed. An attachment's bearer `access_token` must constant-time-match its stored UUID token. Attachments flagged `public=True` are public by design — link-sharing semantics, the same class as a shared Drive link, bounded as such. Everything else falls to a full ACL and record-rule `check_access('read')` under the caller's own user. The historical guess-the-attachment-ID class is closed at this pin. Report routes are `auth="user"`, and the PDF renderer renders records the requester can already access; no unauthenticated URL-fetch endpoint exists in the report path — the URL-shaped surfaces are admin-authored content, a privileged-content problem rather than an unauthenticated one. One deployment note, not an application flaw: with `--x-sendfile`, a misconfigured non-`internal` nginx alias can expose the raw filestore tree by content-checksum name. That is the classic Odoo deployment trap, recorded so it is not mistaken for a code finding.

**The module channel.** Two different things are called installing a module, and they have different trust properties. The uploaded-zip path is data-only by construction: the `base.import.module` wizard is restricted to the system group, the importer itself raises unless the caller is an administrator, and the extractor is an allowlist — manifest data files in `.xml`, `.csv`, or `.sql`, the `static/` tree, and `i18n/*.po` files, with a 100 MB per-file cap. Python files in the zip are never extracted. An uploaded zip cannot install code; code enters an Odoo server only when an operator places files on the addons path. Nothing auto-updates: `update_list()` scans local manifests and fetches nothing, and every install, upgrade, and uninstall operation carries an admin-asserting, audit-logging decorator. No cron touches modules.

The one weakness in this area is the familiar shape at a smaller scale. The apps-store's in-client download — `button_immediate_install_app`, admin-gated — is a plain HTTPS GET whose response body is trusted as a zip and fed to the data-only importer. No checksum, no signature, no hash exists anywhere in that path, nor anywhere in the module model's install path for local modules either. TLS and the store account are the whole integrity chain. The bound is real and stated: at this pin the in-client path fetches data apps only, and what rides the chain is data, not code. It is the self-grading pattern from the rest of this series, one layer up and with a smaller blast radius.

Entropy across the audited surface is sound: API keys from `os.urandom(20)`, session material from `os.urandom(64)`, the restore preamble token from `secrets.token_hex()` — observed live at the head of the backup's `dump.sql` — attachment tokens from `uuid.uuid4()`, and reset tokens as HMAC constructions that need no per-token randomness at all.

## 7. Population and bounds

**Target bound.** The live proof ran against the official `odoo:19` image and `postgres:16-alpine`, composed as the official documentation composes them, with no custom configuration hardening, on a localhost-only rig under our control. No third-party Odoo instance was enumerated, queried, or tested. The construction and defaults were read at the pinned source: Odoo Community 19.0 at `3828306014f63c07b769f0fb3cfce5eb7f7c50d3`.

**Default-posture bound.** The findings in §§1–3 describe the shipped default posture of the documented install path. The manager is closable: an operator who sets a real `admin_passwd`, or sets `list_db = False`, closes the enumeration and the bootstrap — Odoo's own deployment documentation recommends exactly this. The finding is not that the door cannot be locked. It is that the documented path leaves it open, that the lock it ships with is labeled `admin`, and that the first caller through it gets to change that lock to one of their own. Every claim about the wild rests on the credited campaign reporting, not on any measurement of the installed base made here; the share of live instances still carrying the default is not estimated.

**Reachability bound.** The manager surface must be reachable for the chain to run. It is reachable by default wherever the Odoo HTTP port is exposed, which the campaign record shows is common enough to work at scale; a deployment that firewalls the manager or puts it behind a proxy allowlist breaks the chain at the first step. That is an operator mitigation, not a property of the shipped default.

**API-key bound.** The unscoped default applies to keys minted through the ordinary user path; a caller who deliberately specifies a scope gets a scoped key. The no-expiry construction is confined to the system path. The revocation finding has no such bound: no path in the change or reset flow revokes any key, scoped or not.

**Prior-art bound.** The October 2026 cryptominer campaign's use of the enumeration call, and CVE-2026-25137 for the NixOS variant of the default-password problem, are prior art, credited in place. This paper claims the current-pin construction read and the live bootstrap demonstration on the official image as its contribution, and no more.

## 8. Judgment

Each element of the database-manager construction is individually defensible, and the paper trail for each is a reasonable sentence: a first-run bootstrap so an operator can set a password; a listing endpoint so the login page can offer a database picker; a commented example line in a sample configuration. Composed on the install path people actually follow, they produce an instance whose most powerful surface is unauthenticated, whose databases are enumerable by anyone, and whose master credential belongs to the first caller who asks for it with a replacement in hand.

The concentration is what makes it fleet-shaped rather than instance-shaped. One secret — static, server-wide, shipped as a dictionary word — stands in front of backup, restore, and deletion for *every company on the box*, in a product whose ordinary deployment is many companies per box, run by small firms for other small firms. The campaign reporting shows the enumeration half of this is already a worked routine in the wild. The bootstrap half means the routine does not end at learning the database's name.

Beside it, the API-key findings are quieter and more chronic. The key custody at rest is genuinely good; the lifecycle is not. A default key that is secretly the whole account, an administrative path to keys that never expire, and a password reset that revokes none of them together mean the standard incident response — "we changed the password" — silently does not remediate the credential most likely to have been taken. Odoo's own session construction proves the revocation-by-construction pattern was available in the same codebase. The keys simply never got it.

## 9. Fixes

1. **Ship the manager closed.** The official image and the documented compose pattern should set a generated master password at first boot and print it to the operator once — the pattern other products in this series already use — or refuse to serve the manager until `admin_passwd` is set to a non-default value. A commented example line is not a default; the code default behind it is, and it is `admin`.
2. **Remove the silent bootstrap from the network surface.** First-caller password replacement, if it exists at all, belongs at the console or behind proof of the current password — never as a side effect of an unauthenticated mutating call. While the password is the shipped default, mutating manager operations should fail, loudly, rather than adopt the caller's password and proceed.
3. **Default `list_db` to off.** Database-name enumeration should be an operator's explicit choice, not the factory state. The login picker can function with a configured database or a typed name; the campaign record shows what the default is used for.
4. **Scope keys by default.** UI-minted API keys should carry a real scope — RPC at minimum as an explicit, visible choice — and the minting flow should say what an unscoped key is: the account itself. The system path's persistent no-expiry keys should be listed, labeled, and auditable wherever keys are managed.
5. **Revoke keys on password change and reset.** The session layer already binds its tokens to the password hash so revocation is automatic. API keys need the equivalent: a password change or reset should revoke, or at minimum force re-confirmation of, the account's outstanding keys. A reset that leaves keys standing is a remediation that does not remediate.
6. **Give the store download an anchor.** The apps-store data-app download should verify a publisher-supplied checksum or signature before the importer sees the bytes. TLS authenticates the channel; it does not authenticate the content against a compromised endpoint. Data-only bounds the damage today; the anchor is cheap and the pattern, elsewhere in this series, is exactly how data channels grow into code channels.

## Disclosure

At signing, no vendor notice has been sent and this paper has not been published. The audit record — pinned source, notes, live-proof transcripts, and the rig's preserved artifacts — is retained with the audit record.

